= July 2024 — The absent deputy

The board shows **[+ abortion_vote_yes +] yes · [+ abortion_vote_no +] no · [+
abortion_vote_abstain +] abstain**. It does not show **Roman Giertych**, who is
not in the chamber and does not vote.

The KO club's discipline commission suspends his membership rights until **15
October**. That is the whole penalty: he remains a deputy, he remains chairman
of the reckoning team, and he continues to file notifications with prosecutors
throughout the suspension. The club's explanation is that an unexcused absence
from a declared party vote has consequences, which it does, in the form of not
being allowed to attend club meetings for eleven weeks.

[? if not abortion_vote_passed: The arithmetic is what makes this unbearable.
The bill failed. Every absence in that column is a vote that did not need to be
won from PSL, and one of them belongs to the man whose entire public function
is to demonstrate that this government keeps its promises.?]
[? if abortion_vote_passed: The bill survived without him, which is the only
reason this is a discipline story rather than a catastrophe. It also means the
majority that carried it was assembled from deputies who did turn up, none of
whom are running a coordinating body on the government's behalf.?]

**Anna Maria Żukowska** does not need to write a new statement. She wrote it in
2023: nobody put him on the senate pact because nobody wanted to own his votes
on partnerships and abortion. This is what owning them looks like.

Tusk's office briefs that the suspension settles it and that the reckoning team
has a fuller calendar than the discipline commission. That is also true, and it
is the point.

- @giertych_absence_price
- @giertych_absence_code
- @giertych_absence_recruit
- @giertych_absence_accept

@giertych_absence_price
title: Make the absence the headline of the defeat
subtitle: Force KO to defend the empty seat for a week; the cost is that the campaign is about one man rather than the bill.
on-arrival: giertych_line = "Absence campaign"; giertych_standing -= 7; media_capacity += 4; progressive_credibility += 4; feminist_trust += 5; ko_relation -= 6; ko_coalition_dissent += 4; left_poll_momentum += 0.25; ko_poll_momentum -= 0.2; reckoning_noise += 4; news_headline = "The Left makes an empty seat the record of the decriminalisation vote"

Our press team puts the seating plan on every screen with one chair marked, and
keeps it there. Journalists ask KO's spokespeople the same question for six
days, and the answers get worse: a scheduling conflict, then a legal
obligation, then that the vote had a known outcome anyway.

We win the week. We also teach every editor that Lewica's abortion politics is
now a story about Roman Giertych, which is the frame in which it is easiest to
ignore what the bill actually said.

- @poland_event_queue: Put the seating plan on screen

@giertych_absence_code
title: Demand a coalition rule: no rights-vote absentee runs a coalition body
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. A general rule, written and dull, that will still exist when this quarrel is forgotten.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: There is no free capacity to negotiate and police a written attendance rule.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; giertych_line = "Attendance rule for coalition bodies"; giertych_standing -= 6; reckoning_delivery += 4; government_procedural_restraint += 3; institutional_trust += 2; party_unity += 2; ko_relation -= 3; progressive_credibility += 3; news_headline = "The coalition writes an attendance rule for its own coordinating bodies"

We do not ask for his removal. We ask for a rule: a deputy who does not vote on
a declared coalition bill on rights or the courts does not chair a body that
speaks for the coalition on rights or the courts, for as long as the suspension
lasts.

KO signs it because refusing a rule about attendance is impossible in public.
The rule will one day be quoted at one of our own deputies, and we will deserve
it. Giertych keeps the chair by the length of his suspension and knows exactly
who put a clock on it.

- @poland_event_queue: Write the attendance rule

@giertych_absence_recruit
title: Go around him: co-sign the next bill with KO's own progressives
subtitle: Build the majority that was missing instead of prosecuting the deputy who was missing.
on-arrival: giertych_line = "Cross-party progressive bloc"; ko_social_liberal_share += 3; ko_classical_liberal_share -= 3; giertych_standing -= 3; feminist_trust += 6; progressive_credibility += 5; ko_relation += 2; progressives_dissent -= 4; razem_cooperation += 3; movement_autonomy += 3; news_headline = "Left and KO progressives put their names on one rights bill"

Barbara Nowacka's people and ours put the next clinical-access text out with
both sets of signatures on the first page, and we ask the women's organisations
to count deputies in public rather than parties.

It does nothing about Giertych, which is the criticism our own left makes of
it. It does move three or four KO deputies from a private position to a
published one, and published positions are the only kind that can be held to.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the joint text

@giertych_absence_accept
title: Accept the suspension as sufficient and protect the coalition
subtitle: The government survives the week intact and the Left's own voters read the archive later.
on-arrival: giertych_line = "Suspension accepted"; giertych_standing += 5; ko_relation += 5; government_coalition_dissent -= 4; progressive_credibility -= 6; feminist_trust -= 4; progressives_dissent += 6; razem_dissent += 5; public_trust -= 1; news_headline = "The Left treats KO's internal penalty as the end of the matter"

We say that the club imposed a penalty, that the penalty is a matter for the
club, and that the coalition has a housing bill next week. The Chancellery is
grateful and the coalition council is quiet.

The women's organisations that spent April counting deputies for us notice that
we counted this one differently. It is a small entry, and it goes in the same
file as every other week we chose the government over the reason we joined it.

- @poland_event_queue: Move to the housing bill
